  "guideStepDescription": "{step, select, earthquake1{While the ground shakes, get under a table, hold on to its legs, and protect your head.} earthquake2{Shut off the gas valve and electricity, and open the door to secure an exit.} earthquake3{Don't take the elevator, and keep away from buildings and walls as you move.} earthquake4{Protect your head with a bag or similar, and move to an open area such as a sports field or park.} earthquake5{Check emergency alerts and the radio for information, and don't believe unconfirmed rumors.} heavyRain1{Check emergency alerts, TV, and radio for heavy rain warnings and evacuation notices.} heavyRain2{Leave riverside paths, valleys, and breakwaters immediately.} heavyRain3{Once water starts entering an underground parking lot or semi-basement, doors may not open, so go above ground right away.} heavyRain4{Even if the current looks weak, don't walk or drive through it.} heavyRain5{Turn off power and gas, and go to the nearest shelter.} typhoon1{During a typhoon warning, stay indoors and keep checking weather updates.} typhoon2{Fasten window frames, and stay away from glass that could break.} typhoon3{Bring in or tie down things that could blow away, such as flower pots and signs.} typhoon4{You could be swept away by high waves or hurt by falling objects.} typhoon5{Have a flashlight, a power bank, and drinking water ready.} heatWave1{Drink water regularly, even if you don't feel thirsty.} heatWave2{Cut back on going out and outdoor work during the hottest hours, 2 to 5 PM.} heatWave3{Wear loose, light-colored clothes, and use a nearby cooling center.} heatWave4{If you have a headache, dizziness, or nausea, rest somewhere cool, and call 119 if symptoms continue.} heatWave5{Check on neighbors who are vulnerable to heat, such as older adults and children.} other{}}",

